   "If a removable quotient grows only like `‖z‖^(1/2)`, apply Cauchy's derivative estimate on circles of arbitrarily large radius; the derivative bound tends to zero even though ordinary bounded Liouville does not apply."
   :how-to-apply
   ["From the lower bound on `f`, first prove `f z ≠ 0` away from the origin."
    "Define `q z = 0` at the origin and `q z = z²/f z` elsewhere."
    "Use the lower bound to prove `‖q z‖ ≤ ‖z‖^(1/2)` and hence continuity at zero."
    "Combine differentiability on the punctured neighbourhood with continuity at zero via the removable-singularity theorem to make `q` entire."
    "Apply the Cauchy derivative estimate to `q` on a circle of radius `R`; the growth bound gives `‖q'(z)‖ ≤ R^(1/2)/R` up to the fixed-centre adjustment."
    "Let `R→∞` to force every derivative to zero, then use derivative-zero constancy."
    "Evaluate the constant quotient at selected points to contradict the original nonvanishing lower bound."]
